# Embeddings Are Overpriced?

Code, data, checkpoints and every evaluation answer for the paper.

**Paper:** [paper.pdf](https://huggingface.co/QLNI/embeddings-are-overpriced/resolve/main/docs/paper.pdf) (LaTeX source: [paper.tex](https://huggingface.co/QLNI/embeddings-are-overpriced/blob/main/docs/paper.tex)). Preprint DOI: [10.5281/zenodo.23382634](https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23382634). arXiv: [2610.22428](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22428) (cs.CL).

We trained three Gemma-3-270M-shaped language models from scratch on the same 2.05 billion FineWeb-Edu tokens, in the same order, with the same seed. They differ only in the vocabulary and the body.

| arm | vocabulary | body | trainable | file |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **A** | learned tied table, 262,144 x 640 | float | 268.1M | 543 MB (GGUF f16) |
| **B** | **frozen 512-bit codes**, 16.8 MB, no trainable parameters | float | 101.0M | no kernel |
| **C** | frozen 512-bit codes | ternary (BitNet b1.58) | 101.2M | **46 MB** (our kernel) |

The frozen table gives each token one 512-bit code. We computed it once from Gemma 3 270M's embedding (the top 512 principal directions, then the sign of each), and the same codes serve the input and the output. We add a fixed per-token prior to the output scores: the log unigram frequency of each token in the training data, which we call the seal. Two small linear maps, 512 to 640 on the way in and 640 to 512 on the way out, connect the codes to the body.

Everything the paper used is here, so any number in it can be checked: the training script with every setting, the table, the data with checksums, all checkpoints (every 800 steps, with optimiser state), the per-step logs, the evaluation scripts, every per-question result, and the CPU kernel.

## Results

Zero-shot accuracy in %, with the 95% bootstrap range over questions in brackets. Validation loss is in nats per token on 512 held-out sequences.

| | LAMBADA | HellaSwag | ARC-Easy | PIQA | val loss |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| A learned table, float | 21.6 (20.5-22.7) | 30.4 (29.4-31.2) | 45.9 (43.9-47.9) | 60.6 (58.3-62.8) | 3.155 |
| B frozen table, float | 23.2 (22.1-24.4) | 30.4 (29.4-31.2) | 42.5 (40.5-44.5) | 61.5 (59.2-63.8) | 3.358 |
| C frozen table, ternary | 22.5 (21.3-23.6) | 29.4 (28.6-30.3) | 41.0 (39.0-43.0) | 59.5 (57.2-61.7) | 3.439 |

Compared question by question, B beats A on LAMBADA by 1.6 points, ties on HellaSwag and PIQA, and loses ARC-Easy by 3.4. C ties A on LAMBADA and PIQA and loses HellaSwag by 0.9 and ARC-Easy by 4.9. So the learned table costs 2.6 times the trainable parameters and buys 0.20 nats of loss and one benchmark.

On words seen 10 to 99 times in training, B and C are 1.7 to 2.3 nats better than A. On words seen a million times or more, A is ahead by 0.035 nats. Of Gemma's 262,144 tokens, 52,782 never appear in our training data. In A their rows stay random; in B and C they still carry Gemma's codes.

We also added 10,000 biomedical words to the vocabulary after training, without any further training. A got 42.9% worse, B 3.3% and C 3.1% (nats per character). The tests where the frozen table did not help are reported as well, in `eval/CONTROL_TOKENS.md` and `eval/emoji_*.json`.

### On a laptop CPU

Intel Xeon W-11955M, 8 threads, greedy decoding, 128-token context, 16-bit KV cache for both. Arm A runs in llama.cpp b11515 from the GGUF files in `gguf/`. Arm C runs in `kernel/cgemma.c`.

| model | runtime | file | peak RAM | tokens/s | perplexity (300k val tokens, ctx 512) |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| A | llama.cpp, GGUF f16 | 543 MB | 723 MB | 62 | 23.86 |
| A | llama.cpp, GGUF Q8_0 | 292 MB | 471 MB | 116 | 23.86 |
| A | llama.cpp, GGUF Q4_K_M | 253 MB | 446 MB | 130 | 23.97 |
| **C** | **our kernel** | **46 MB** | **62 MB** | **615 to 740** | 31.2 |

The 8-bit GGUF loses nothing against f16, so we compare C with A at Q8_0. C generates 5.3 to 6.4 times faster in 7.6 times less memory. A cannot go below 446 MB at any quantisation, because its 16-bit table alone is 250 MB. C's loss is 0.28 nats higher. We did not write a kernel for B.

