## Using the models

**Arm A** is a standard Gemma 3 270M and loads anywhere: `Gemma3ForCausalLM.from_pretrained("final/armA_hf")`, or any GGUF file in `gguf/` with llama.cpp.

**Arms B and C** need this repository's code, because a frozen code table with a seal is not a Hugging Face layer type.

```python
import numpy as np, torch, sys; sys.path.insert(0, "code")
import fastc                                             # loads a checkpoint; for C it rounds the ternary weights once
counts = np.load("tables/unigram_counts_fineweb_edu_2.05B.npy")
model, _ = fastc.load("C", "C/step_07820.pt", counts)    # or ("B", "B/step_07820.pt", counts)
ids = torch.tensor([[2, 651, 6037, 576, 1385, 603]])     # Gemma 3 tokenizer ids, <bos> first
with torch.no_grad():
    logits = model.logits(model.hidden(ids))              # [1, 6, 262144]
```

Set `SAVED_TABLE=1` to read the table from `tables/` instead of rebuilding it from Gemma 3 270M. The rebuild gives the same bits; the file just saves the download. `final/armB` and `final/armC` hold the final weights without the optimiser state and without the table, which lives in `tables/`.

**Arm C on a CPU**, no Python needed:

```
gcc -O3 -mavx2 -mfma -mf16c -DNT=8 -o cgemma kernel/cgemma.c -lm -lpthread      # add -lpsapi on Windows
PIN=1 ./cgemma kernel/armC.bin bench 128 64             # prefill 128 tokens, generate 64; prints tokens/s and peak RAM
./cgemma kernel/armC.bin golden kernel/ids.txt out.bin  # logits for the check against PyTorch (kernel/golden_c.py)
```

The kernel is a single C file. It does ternary matrix-vector products with int8 activations, attention with a 16-bit KV cache, and a readout that scores all 262,144 codes straight off the 1-bit table. The readout does a quick int8 pass over every row, then an exact second pass only over the rows that can still win. Its top token matches the PyTorch model at every position we tested.

## Where the frozen table comes from

We shipped this idea before we tested it properly. Two earlier QLNI models have no learned embedding at all; every token is a fixed 512-bit code.

| model | what it is | code | weights |
|---|---|---|---|
| **SHADOW 250M Instruct** | 250M parameters, 131,072-token frozen table, body under 2 bits per weight, 100M-token archive on disk. 60 MB with the vocabulary, about 400 tokens/s and 80 MB RAM on a laptop CPU. Held-out perplexity 23.3; PIQA 0.60, HellaSwag 0.34, ARC-Easy 0.41 | [github.com/QLNI/SHADOW-250M-Instruct](https://github.com/QLNI/SHADOW-250M-Instruct) | [huggingface.co/QLNI/SHADOW-250M](https://huggingface.co/QLNI/SHADOW-250M) |
| **SHADOW 50M Instruct** | 44M ternary parameters, 73,880-token frozen table, exact circuits for arithmetic, dates and units inside the forward pass, memory on disk. 19.8 MB, about 2,000 tokens/s in 40 MB RAM. Also runs in the browser | [github.com/QLNI/SHADOW-50M-Instruct](https://github.com/QLNI/SHADOW-50M-Instruct) | [huggingface.co/QLNI/shadow-50m-instruct](https://huggingface.co/QLNI/shadow-50m-instruct) |

Both are MIT-licensed and both work, but neither was a controlled experiment. Each changed many things at once, and neither was ever trained with a learned table for comparison. This repository is that comparison: same body, same data, same steps, same seed, and only the vocabulary changes. The CPU kernel here is ported from the SHADOW 50M kernel.

## What comes next

A frozen table does not have to stop at words. In the next paper the same frozen code space carries text, images, audio and video into one small body, with no learned embedding anywhere, still running on a CPU. The numbers will come with that paper.

## Reproducing the training

`code/prep_data.py` tokenises FineWeb-Edu (or use `data/`, with checksums in `data/SHA256SUMS.txt`), then `python code/train.py --arm A|B|C` runs with the settings in the script: 2.05B tokens, sequence length 1,024, 262,144 tokens per step, 7,820 steps, AdamW 6e-4 with cosine decay to 10% for A and B, the BitNet two-stage schedule in `code/bitnet_schedule.json` for C, seed 0 and the same data order. One arm takes about 17 GPU-hours on an RTX 5090. For evaluation run `code/eval.py`, then `code/bars.py` for the paired bootstrap.

